      <article class="toc-content prose">
    <p><strong>Last updated:</strong> April 25, 2026</p>
    <p><strong>Conformance target:</strong> Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2, Level AA, plus Section 508.</p>
    <p><strong>Conformance status:</strong> partially conformant. Most of the site fully meets the standard, and the known limitations are listed below.</p>

    <h2 id="my-commitment">My commitment</h2>
    <p>Accessibility is a baseline on every site I design and build, including this one. My working standard is WCAG 2.2 Level AA, the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines from the W3C. It covers color contrast, keyboard navigation, screen-reader support, visible focus, motion, language, and clear labels. I write the same standard into my client agreements, so it's a contractual commitment, not an aspiration.</p>
    <p>Beyond the legal minimum, I treat accessibility as a mark of quality. A site that fails screen-reader users usually also fails search-engine crawlers, color-blind visitors, people using phone keyboards, and aging eyes. Building accessibly means building well.</p>

    <h2 id="what-i-do-on-every-site">What I do on every site</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Measured contrast ratios.</strong> I check every text and background pair against WCAG AA (4.5:1 for normal text, and 3:1 for large text and interface parts) with the TPGi Colour Contrast Analyser. That's every pair, in every theme.</li>
      <li><strong>Full keyboard navigation.</strong> Every interactive control can be reached with <kbd>Tab</kbd> and used with the keyboard alone. Focus rings are visible (a 3 px gold ring at a 3 px offset) and predictable, and the tab order matches the visual order.</li>
      <li><strong>Semantic HTML.</strong> Headings are in order, landmarks are labeled, lists are coded as lists, and buttons are coded as buttons, so screen readers get a clean structure to move through by region or heading.</li>
      <li><strong>Skip links.</strong> "Skip to content" is the first focusable element on every page, so keyboard and screen-reader users can jump past the navigation.</li>
      <li><strong>Landmarks.</strong> Each region of the page (<code>header</code>, <code>nav</code>, <code>main</code>, <code>aside</code>, and <code>footer</code>) is a proper HTML5 landmark, with an <code>aria-label</code> where there's more than one of a kind.</li>
      <li><strong>Image alt text.</strong> Decorative SVGs are hidden from assistive technology with <code>aria-hidden="true"</code> and <code>focusable="false"</code>. Meaningful images get descriptive alt text, and complex images (charts and diagrams) get long descriptions.</li>
      <li><strong>Reduced motion respected.</strong> Animations honor <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>. If your device asks for less motion, the site turns off transitions and movement effects.</li>
      <li><strong>Forms with proper labels.</strong> Every field has a visible label, not just placeholder text. Errors are announced through <code>role="alert"</code> and explained in plain language, with a way to fix them.</li>
      <li><strong>Light and dark themes.</strong> Color pairs meet the contrast targets in both modes. The toggle has three states (Light, System, and Dark), and your choice is saved between visits.</li>
      <li><strong>Touch targets of 44 x 44 CSS pixels.</strong> Interactive elements are comfortably large on a phone, and the spacing between neighboring targets prevents accidental taps.</li>
      <li><strong>Forced-colors mode (Windows High Contrast).</strong> The site stays readable when the operating system forces its own colors, and I use <code>forced-colors: active</code> media queries where a component's styling needs to give way to system colors.</li>
      <li><strong>Text for screen readers only</strong>, through the <code>.sr-only</code> utility class, for context that's obvious on screen but missing when a screen reader reads the page in order.</li>
      <li><strong>Language attribute</strong> set on <code>&lt;html lang="en"&gt;</code>, so screen readers use the right pronunciation.</li>
      <li><strong>Color is never the only way meaning is shown.</strong> Status badges pair color with an icon or a label, and required form fields have an asterisk plus a visible label suffix.</li>
      <li><strong>aria-current="page"</strong> on the active menu link, so screen-reader users know where they are.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="wcag-2-2-highlights-we-specifically-test">WCAG 2.2 criteria I test specifically</h2>
    <p>WCAG 2.2 added nine new criteria to version 2.1. I test the ones that matter most for a marketing site:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (AA).</strong> The focused element is never fully hidden under a sticky header, a pop-up, or a cookie banner.</li>
      <li><strong>2.4.12 Focus Not Obscured (AAA).</strong> No part of the focused element is hidden. I treat this as a firm target on this site.</li>
      <li><strong>2.5.7 Dragging Movements (AA).</strong> No interaction depends on dragging. Where I use carousels, they have buttons as an alternative.</li>
      <li><strong>2.5.8 Target Size Minimum (AA).</strong> At least 44 x 44 px on every interactive control.</li>
      <li><strong>3.3.7 Redundant Entry (A).</strong> If a multi-step form would ask for the same information twice, I fill it in for you or skip the second request.</li>
      <li><strong>3.3.8 Accessible Authentication (AA).</strong> Not applicable, because the site has no user logins.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="how-i-test">How I test</h2>
    <p>On every site, before launch and after every significant change, I run through these checks:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Automated audits</strong> with three checkers: Google's audit built into Chrome DevTools, axe DevTools, and WAVE. A site launches only with zero critical and zero serious issues.</li>
      <li><strong>Keyboard-only navigation</strong> from the first focusable element to the last, on every page. Every interactive control must be reachable, usable, and visibly focused.</li>
      <li><strong>Screen-reader testing</strong> with VoiceOver on macOS and iOS, and NVDA on Windows. I listen to every page from top to bottom to catch problems with landmarks, headings, and labels.</li>
      <li><strong>Browser zoom</strong> up to 200%, with no sideways scrolling and no clipped content.</li>
      <li><strong>Contrast checks</strong> on every text and background pair, in both light and dark themes.</li>
      <li><strong>Reduced-motion checks</strong> with the operating-system setting turned on, confirming that every animation and transition stops.</li>
      <li><strong>Forced-colors testing</strong> in Windows High Contrast mode.</li>
      <li><strong>Manual review</strong> of every new component for heading order, label connections, focus order, ARIA use, and meaning that doesn't depend on color.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="known-limitations">Known limitations</h2>
    <p>These are the limitations I know about on this site today:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Demo sites under <code>/demo/</code></strong> follow most of my accessibility approach, but each one shows off a different typographic and visual style for a different industry. Each demo's own accessibility statement lists any differences that come from its design.</li>
      <li><strong>Outside components</strong> (the site search interface and the glossary tooltips). I've set both to use accessible defaults, but their internal code is maintained by others. I watch their releases and check each upgrade.</li>
      <li><strong>PDF documents</strong> (if the site links to any) aren't always tagged for screen readers. Wherever I link to a PDF, I provide an HTML version or a plain-text summary.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If you find anything else on the site that doesn't work for you, please tell me. Accessibility issues skip the queue, and I handle them quickly on most business days.</p>

    <h2 id="standards-we-conform-to">Standards I conform to</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>WCAG 2.2 Level AA</strong>: the W3C's accessibility standard.</li>
      <li><strong>Section 508</strong> of the U.S. Rehabilitation Act, as updated in 2018 to align with WCAG 2.0 AA.</li>
      <li><strong>EN 301 549</strong>: the European accessibility standard for information and communication technology, which references WCAG 2.1 AA. My WCAG 2.2 AA target goes beyond it.</li>
      <li><strong>ADA Title III</strong>: the ADA doesn't formally name WCAG, but U.S. courts have routinely treated WCAG AA as the de facto standard. My work is built to that level.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="assistive-technologies-i-test-against">Assistive technology I test with</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Screen readers</strong>: VoiceOver (macOS and iOS), NVDA (Windows), and TalkBack (Android, when a build calls for it).</li>
      <li><strong>Magnification</strong>: macOS Zoom, Windows Magnifier, and browser zoom up to 400%.</li>
      <li><strong>Speech input</strong>: Voice Control (macOS and iOS) and Dragon NaturallySpeaking (Windows).</li>
      <li><strong>Keyboard only</strong>: every interaction tested without a mouse or other pointing device.</li>
    </ul>

